  nfl: {
    menu: 'Voluntary', title: 'Voluntary prayers (Nafl)',
    note: 'Voluntary (nafl) prayers are not a must – they are a gift to yourself. Don’t pray them at the disliked times: while the sun is rising, when it stands at its highest at midday, and while it is setting (for Ḥanafīs also after the Fajr and after the ʿAṣr prayer). You’ll find Tarāwīḥ and Witr under “More prayers”.',
    when: {
      tarawih: 'Tarāwīḥ is prayed only in Ramadan: every night after the Isha prayer, before Witr. It is prayed in units of 2 rakʿahs, each ending with salām – here you practise one such unit. Most communities (Ḥanafī, Shāfiʿī, Ḥanbalī, and also Diyanet) pray 20 rakʿahs, i.e. this unit ten times; others pray 8 (following a report from ʿĀʾisha). After every 4 rakʿahs you sit and rest for a moment – hence the name “Tarāwīḥ” (rests). During this pause people praise Allah, in Turkey for example with “Subḥāna dhi l-mulki wa l-malakūt …” or with ṣalawāt. At the end, in Ramadan, the congregation usually prays Witr together too. Praying in the mosque is especially lovely, but praying alone at home is just as valid.',
      tahajjud: 'Tahajjud is the night prayer after sleeping: sleep a little after the Isha prayer, then get up before Fajr. The best time is the last third of the night – then, as Bukhārī reports, Allah asks: “Who is calling upon Me, that I may answer him?” You pray 2 to 12 rakʿahs, always in pairs, with long recitation and long prostrations. If you are sure you will wake up, pray Witr afterwards as the last prayer of the night. (Qur’an 17:79)',
      duha: 'The Ḍuḥā prayer is prayed in the forenoon: from about 45 minutes after sunrise, when the sun has risen “the length of a spear”, until shortly before midday (about 15 minutes before Dhuhr). 2, 4 or 8 rakʿahs in pairs. According to a ḥadīth in Muslim, it is like a charity for every joint in your body – a thank-you for a new day.',
      ishraq: 'Ishrāq is the early Ḍuḥā prayer: whoever prays Fajr in congregation, stays seated remembering Allah until the sun has risen, and then prays 2 rakʿahs, has a reward like that of a Hajj and an Umrah (Tirmidhī). Wait until the sun has fully risen (about 15–20 minutes after sunrise; in Turkey 45 minutes is often given) – you do not pray while it is rising.',
      awwabin: 'Awwābīn (“those who often turn back to Allah”) is, for many Ḥanafīs, the voluntary prayer between Maghrib and Isha: after the Sunnah of Maghrib, up to 6 rakʿahs in three units of 2. Other scholars call the Ḍuḥā prayer the “prayer of the Awwābīn”. Both are good – a quiet moment in the evening.',
      wuduprayer: 'Right after wuḍūʾ – before your limbs are dry – pray 2 rakʿahs. The Prophet heard Bilāl’s footsteps in Paradise, because Bilāl prayed after every ablution (Bukhārī). Ḥanafī: not at the disliked times, so for example not after the ʿAṣr prayer.',
      istikhara: 'You pray Istikhāra when you have to choose between permissible things – school, work, moving house, marriage. 2 rakʿahs, for example with al-Kāfirūn in the first and al-Ikhlāṣ in the second. Then comes the supplication: where it says “this matter”, think of your question. You don’t have to wait for a dream: decide calmly where your heart leans, and ask people you trust. You can repeat it several times.',
      hajat: 'You pray the prayer of need when something weighs on you or you wish for something very much – health, an exam, a worry in the family. 2 rakʿahs (some pray 4), then praise Allah, say the ṣalawāt and ask in your own words. The reports about a fixed form are weak, but a voluntary prayer with a supplication is always good.',
      tawba: 'You pray the prayer of repentance when you regret a mistake. Abū Bakr reported: whoever commits a sin, then performs ablution, prays 2 rakʿahs and asks Allah for forgiveness, Allah forgives him (Abū Dāwūd, Tirmidhī; Qur’an 3:135). True repentance means: feeling sorry, stopping, resolving not to do it again – and if you have wronged someone, putting it right with them.',
      tasbihp: 'The Tasbīḥ prayer has 4 rakʿahs with 300 glorifications in total – 75 in each rakʿah. The Prophet taught it to his uncle ʿAbbās: every day if possible, otherwise every Friday, every month, every year – or at least once in a lifetime (Abū Dāwūd, Ibn Mājah). Many pray it on the Kandil nights and on Laylat al-Qadr. Count on your fingers, not out loud. If you forget a count, make it up at the next position – but not in the short standing after rukūʿ and not in the sitting between the prostrations. Don’t pray it at the disliked times.',
    },
    duaTitle: { istikhara: 'Supplication of Istikhāra', hajat: 'Supplication in need', tawba: 'Sayyid al-Istighfār' },
    duaInstr: {
      istikhara: 'After the salām: raise your hands, praise Allah and say the ṣalawāt. Then make this supplication. At “this matter” (hādha l-amr) think of your question – or say it. Afterwards decide calmly, and also ask people you trust.',
      hajat: 'After the salām: raise your hands, praise Allah, say the ṣalawāt and ask in your own words for what you need. The supplication of the Prophet Yūnus from the Qur’an fits every worry.',
      tawba: 'After the salām: raise your hands and sincerely ask Allah for forgiveness – with the “master of supplications for forgiveness” (Sayyid al-Istighfār). Feel sorry, stop, and resolve not to do it again.',
    },
  },
  tsp: {
    title: (n) => `Tasbīḥ ${n}×`,
    at: {
      before: (n) => `After the opening supplication (Subḥānaka), before the Fātiḥa: quietly say ${n}× “Subḥāna llāhi wa l-ḥamdu li-llāhi wa lā ilāha illa llāhu wa llāhu akbar”. Count on your fingers.`,
      sura: (n) => `After the sūra, still standing and before rukūʿ: quietly say the glorification ${n}×.`,
      ruku: (n) => `Stay in rukūʿ: after “Subḥāna rabbiya l-ʿaẓīm” (3×), say the glorification ${n}×.`,
      itidal: (n) => `Standing upright, after “Rabbanā wa laka l-ḥamd”: the glorification ${n}×.`,
      sujud: (n) => `Stay in prostration: after “Subḥāna rabbiya l-aʿlā” (3×), say the glorification ${n}×.`,
      jalsa: (n) => `Sitting between the two prostrations: the glorification ${n}×.`,
      after: (n) => `After the second prostration, sit up briefly (before standing up or before the tashahhud) and say the glorification ${n}×.`,
    },
    school: {
      hanafi: 'Ḥanafī (following ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak): 4 rakʿahs with one salām. In each rakʿah 15× before the Fātiḥa, 10× after the sūra, and 10× each in rukūʿ, standing after it, in both prostrations and sitting between them – 75 per rakʿah, 300 in total.',
      shafii: 'Shāfiʿī (following the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿAbbās): 15× after the sūra, 10× each in rukūʿ, standing after it, in both prostrations, sitting between them, and once more 10× in the short sitting after the second prostration – 75 per rakʿah. By day the four are prayed together or as 2 + 2, by night as 2 + 2.',
      maliki: 'In the Mālikī school the Tasbīḥ prayer is not widely practised; those who pray it mostly follow the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿAbbās – as shown here, as 2 + 2 rakʿahs.',
      hanbali: 'Imam Aḥmad considered the report weak; many scholars (including Ḥanafīs and Shāfiʿīs) do recommend the prayer, however. The form after Ibn ʿAbbās is shown.',
    },
  },
